{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_release-process","statement":"Django versions are numbered A.B or A.B.C, where A.B is the feature release number and C is the patch release number.","source_span":"Versions are numbered in the form ``A.B`` or ``A.B.C``."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_release-process","statement":"Patch releases (C) are incremented for bugfix and security releases and are 100% backwards-compatible with the previous patch release, except when a security or data loss issue can't be fixed without breaking compatibility.","source_span":"``C`` is the *patch release* version number, which is incremented for bugfix and security releases. These releases will be 100% backwards-compatible with the previous patch release."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_release-process","statement":"Before a new feature release, alpha, beta, and release candidate releases are made in the form A.B alpha/beta/rc N.","source_span":"we'll make alpha, beta, and release candidate releases. These are of the form ``A.B alpha/beta/rc N``"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_release-process","statement":"Each Django release series has its own branch called stable/A.B.x, from which bugfix/security releases are issued.","source_span":"each release series has its own branch, called ``stable/A.B.x``, and bugfix/security releases will be issued from those branches"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_release-process","statement":"Django feature releases happen roughly every eight months.","source_span":"Feature releases (A.B, A.B+1, etc.) will happen roughly every eight months"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_release-process","statement":"Long-term support (LTS) releases get security and data loss fixes for a guaranteed period, typically three years.","source_span":"These releases will get security and data loss fixes applied for a guaranteed period of time, typically three years."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_release-process","statement":"Starting with Django 2.0, version numbers follow a loose semantic versioning scheme, e.g. 2.0, 2.1, 2.2 (LTS), 3.0, 3.1, 3.2 (LTS).","source_span":"Starting with Django 2.0, version numbers will use a loose form of `semantic versioning`... For example: 2.0, 2.1, 2.2 (LTS), 3.0, 3.1, 3.2 (LTS), etc."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_release-process","statement":"Deprecation shims are kept for at least two feature releases.","source_span":"our policy of keeping deprecation shims for at least two feature releases"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_release-process","statement":"In the example, a feature deprecated in Django 4.2 raises RemovedInDjango51Warning, still works as a compatible replica in Django 5.0, and is removed outright in Django 5.1.","source_span":"Django 4.2 will contain a backwards-compatible replica of the function which will raise a ``RemovedInDjango51Warning``... Django 5.1 will remove the feature outright."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_release-process","statement":"Deprecation warnings are silent by default and can be displayed using the python -Wd option.","source_span":"The warnings are silent by default. You can turn on display of these warnings with the ``python -Wd`` option."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_release-process","statement":"Security fixes and data loss bug fixes are applied to the current main branch, the last two feature release branches, and any supported long-term support release branches.","source_span":"Security fixes and data loss bugs will be applied to the current main branch, the last two feature release branches, and any other supported long-term support release branches."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_release-process","statement":"Halfway between the release of Django 5.1 and 5.2, security/data loss fixes applied to main, stable/5.1.x, stable/5.0.x, and stable/4.2.x (LTS) would trigger releases such as 5.1.1, 5.0.5, and 4.2.8.","source_span":"They will trigger the release of ``5.1.1``, ``5.0.5``, ``4.2.8``, etc."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_release-process","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"In Django's version numbering scheme A.B.C, what do the A.B and C parts each represent?","answer":"A.B is the feature release version number, and C is the patch release version number, incremented for bugfix and security releases."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_release-process","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How often does Django typically ship a new feature release?","answer":"Roughly every eight months."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_release-process","fact_refs":[5],"question":"For how long do Django's long-term support (LTS) releases receive security and data loss fixes?","answer":"Typically three years, a guaranteed period set for each LTS release."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_release-process","fact_refs":[6],"question":"According to Django's semantic versioning example starting at version 2.0, which releases in the sequence 2.0, 2.1, 2.2, 3.0, 3.1, 3.2 are designated LTS?","answer":"Version 2.2 and version 3.2 are designated as the LTS releases in that sequence."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_release-process","fact_refs":[8],"question":"If a feature is deprecated starting in Django 4.2, in which version is it removed outright?","answer":"It is removed outright in Django 5.1, after remaining as a backwards-compatible replica through Django 4.2 and 5.0."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_release-process","fact_refs":[10],"question":"Which branches receive security fixes and data-loss bug fixes under Django's supported versions policy?","answer":"The current main branch, the last two feature release branches, and any other supported long-term support (LTS) release branches."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_release-process","fact_refs":[9],"question":"How can a Django developer make deprecation warnings visible, since they are silent by default?","answer":"By running Python with the -Wd option."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_security","statement":"Security issues in Django should be reported by emailing security@djangoproject.com rather than the public Trac instance.","source_span":"please report security issues by emailing security@djangoproject.com"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_security","statement":"Reporters should receive an acknowledgment from a member of the security team within 3 working days of submitting an issue via email.","source_span":"you should receive an acknowledgment from a member of the security team within 3 working days"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_security","statement":"It can take several weeks before the security team comes to a conclusion on a reported issue.","source_span":"It can take several weeks before the security team comes to a conclusion."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_security","statement":"All Django security reports aim to be resolved within the industry-standard 90 days.","source_span":"All reports aim to be resolved within the industry-standard 90 days."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_security","statement":"Confirmed vulnerabilities with a high severity level will be addressed promptly.","source_span":"Confirmed vulnerabilities with a :ref:`high severity level <severity-levels>` will be addressed promptly."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_security","statement":"The public key ID for security@djangoproject.com, used for sending encrypted reports, is 0xfcb84b8d1d17f80b.","source_span":"the public key ID for ``security@djangoproject.com`` is ``0xfcb84b8d1d17f80b``"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_security","statement":"An initial security report should include a brief description, a minimal working proof of concept, the versions of Django and Python tested, and optionally a minimal patch with mitigation.","source_span":"It should include: A brief description of the issue and where in Django it occurs. A minimal, working proof of concept... The versions of Django and Python you tested against. Optionally, a minimal patch"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_security","statement":"Reporters should not include severity scores (CVSS or otherwise), lengthy background sections, multiple headers, or a determination of whether the issue constitutes a vulnerability.","source_span":"Please do not include severity scores (CVSS or otherwise), lengthy background sections, multiple headers, or a determination of whether the issue constitutes a vulnerability."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_security","statement":"Django only officially supports the latest micro release (A.B.C) of Python, so vulnerabilities that only occur on an end-of-life Python version are not considered valid.","source_span":"vulnerabilities that only occur when Django is run on a version of Python that is no longer receiving security updates (\"end-of-life\") are **not considered valid**, even if that version is listed as supported by Django"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_security","statement":"Reports based on a failure to sanitize user input are not considered valid security vulnerabilities, since input handling is the developer's responsibility.","source_span":"Reports based on a failure to sanitize user input are not valid security vulnerabilities. It is the developer's responsibility to properly handle user input."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_security","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What email address should be used to report a security vulnerability found in Django?","answer":"security@djangoproject.com."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_security","fact_refs":[1],"question":"How quickly should someone reporting a Django security issue expect an initial acknowledgment?","answer":"Within 3 working days of submitting the issue by email."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_security","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Within how many days does Django's security team aim to resolve reported vulnerabilities?","answer":"90 days, described as the industry-standard timeframe."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_security","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What is the public key ID for security@djangoproject.com for sending encrypted security reports?","answer":"0xfcb84b8d1d17f80b."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_security","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What four elements should an initial Django security report include?","answer":"A brief description of the issue, a minimal working proof of concept, the versions of Django and Python tested against, and optionally a minimal patch with a mitigation."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_security","fact_refs":[8],"question":"Is a Django vulnerability that only occurs on an end-of-life version of Python considered a valid report?","answer":"No, it is not considered valid even if that Python version is still listed as officially supported by Django."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","statement":"The first step to contributing to Django is forking it on GitHub at https://github.com/django/django/fork.","source_span":"First, `fork Django on GitHub <https://github.com/django/django/fork>`__."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","statement":"A personal fork of Django is cloned with the command git clone https://github.com/YourGitHubName/django.git.","source_span":"$ git clone https://github.com/YourGitHubName/django.git"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","statement":"Adding --depth 1 to the git clone command skips downloading Django's full commit history, reducing data transfer from about 250 MB to about 70 MB.","source_span":"You can add the ``--depth 1`` argument to ``git clone`` to skip downloading all of Django's commit history, which reduces data transfer from ~250 MB to ~70 MB."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","statement":"A new virtual environment for Django development is created with the command python3 -m venv ~/.virtualenvs/djangodev.","source_span":"$ python3 -m venv ~/.virtualenvs/djangodev"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","statement":"The Django development virtual environment is activated with the command source ~/.virtualenvs/djangodev/bin/activate.","source_span":"$ source ~/.virtualenvs/djangodev/bin/activate"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","statement":"The cloned local copy of Django is installed for development in editable mode with python -m pip install -e /path/to/your/local/clone/django/.","source_span":"$ python -m pip install -e /path/to/your/local/clone/django/"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","statement":"The tutorial covers installing Git, downloading Django's development version, running the test suite, writing a test, writing code, testing changes, and submitting a pull request.","source_span":"Installing Git.\n* Downloading a copy of Django's development version.\n* Running Django's test suite.\n* Writing a test for your changes.\n* Writing the code for your changes.\n* Testing your changes.\n* Submitting a pull request."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is the first step to take when contributing to Django according to the tutorial?","answer":"Fork Django on GitHub at https://github.com/django/django/fork."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What git command clones a personal fork of Django from GitHub?","answer":"git clone https://github.com/YourGitHubName/django.git."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","fact_refs":[2],"question":"How much data transfer does adding --depth 1 to the git clone command save when cloning Django, and why?","answer":"It reduces data transfer from about 250 MB to about 70 MB by skipping the download of Django's full commit history."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What command creates the recommended Python virtual environment for Django development?","answer":"python3 -m venv ~/.virtualenvs/djangodev."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What command activates the djangodev virtual environment on macOS or Linux?","answer":"source ~/.virtualenvs/djangodev/bin/activate."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_django_contributing","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How should a locally cloned copy of Django be installed so changes are immediately reflected?","answer":"With python -m pip install -e /path/to/your/local/clone/django/, which installs it in editable mode."}
